#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# tally

#% text
# `tally(xs)` gives the pairs `[x, n]` of each distinct element `x` of the list `xs` and the number of times `n` it occurs.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# The pairs come in the order of first occurrence.

#% item
# Elements are compared by identity: `1` and `1.0` are different.

#% item
# `counts` gives the same as an association from element to count.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
tally([a, b, a, c, b, a])
#% output
#= [[a, 3], [b, 2], [c, 1]]

#% cell
tally(characters("banana"))
#% output
#= [["b", 1], ["a", 3], ["n", 2]]

#% cell
tally([])
#% output
#= []

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Any elements, lists included:

#% cell
tally([[1, 2], [2, 1], [1, 2]])
#% output
#= [[[1, 2], 2], [[2, 1], 1]]

#% text
# A test that two elements count as the same:

#% cell
tally([1, 2, 11, 12, 21], (a, b) => floor(a/10) == floor(b/10))
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [11, 2], [21, 1]]

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The frequencies of the faces of a die in 600 seeded throws:

#% cell
seed(6); sort(tally(random_integer([1, 6], 600)))
#% output
#= [[1, 100], [2, 90], [3, 116], [4, 98], [5, 97], [6, 99]]

#% text
# The most common remainder of the primes below 1000 modulo 4:

#% cell
tally(mod(select(range(1000), is_prime), 4))
#% output
#= [[2, 1], [3, 87], [1, 80]]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The counts add up to the length:

#% cell
xs = characters("mississippi")
total(map(last, tally(xs))) == length(xs)
#% output
#= true

#% text
# `counts` gives an association:

#% cell
counts(xs)
#% output
#= assoc("m" -> 1, "i" -> 4, "s" -> 4, "p" -> 2)

#% text
# The distinct elements are `unique`:

#% cell
map(first, tally(xs)) === unique(xs)
#% output
#= true

#% subsection
# Possible issues

#% text
# Exact and machine numbers of the same value are different elements:

#% cell
tally([1, 1.0, 1])
#% output
#= [[1, 2], [1.0, 1]]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `counts`, `count`, `unique`, `gather`, `histogram_list`
